#
# PROBLEM: one word family, four constructs — and quoting alone
# flips a condition's semantic domain.
#
#   stop              verb: host-side hard power-off, an action
#   stopped           bare condition: backend reports not running
#   machine stopped   header: a precondition, performs nothing
#   "stopped"         quoted: guest SCREEN TEXT saying "stopped"

description "The stop/stopped family"
platform    dos
machine     stopped     # expectation only — no action performed
timeout     30s

start                   # verb: acts now
enter "shutdown"
wait  stopped           # bare word: observes the MACHINE domain
# wait "stopped"        # quoted, the SAME word would watch for the
                        # literal text "stopped" on the guest screen

# One character class — the quotes — silently moves the condition
# from the machine domain to the guest-screen domain. A reader
# skimming a column of wait lines cannot tell a machine-event wait
# from a text wait without focusing on the quotes:
#
#     wait "Formatting complete"      guest screen
#     wait stopped                    machine state
#     wait "stopped"                  guest screen again
#
# The header adds a third reading of the same word: `machine
# stopped` is inert (a precondition), while `stop` is destructive.
# The noun-first/verb-first zone rule carries that whole
# distinction; the words themselves do not.
#
# OPEN QUESTION: should machine-state conditions be namespaced so
# bare condition words cannot shadow screen text in the reader's
# eye? Sketches (all NOT current syntax):
#     wait machine=stopped        # property-flavored, verbose
#     wait <stopped>              # key-token spelling, wrong class
#     wait stopped                # status quo: contextual keyword
# The status quo is defensible (one machine-event word today), but
# each future machine event (paused? rebooted?) widens the bare
# vocabulary and the collision surface with screen text.
